{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '1,100p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\\documentclass{article}\n     2\t% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n     3\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n     4\t\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n     5\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n     6\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n     7\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n     8\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n     9\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n    10\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n    11\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n    12\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n    13\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n    14\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n    15\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n    16\t}\n    17\t% --- end watermark ---\n    18\t\n    19\t\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n    20\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption}\n    21\t\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n    22\t\\title{Scale-Adaptive Conformal Regions:\\\\Reliability and Limits on the Simplex}\n    23\t\\author{}\n    24\t\\newcommand{\\R}{\\mathbb{R}}\n    25\t\\newcommand{\\simplex}{\\Delta^{K-1}_{\\circ}}\n    26\t\\newcommand{\\clip}{\\operatorname{clip}}\n    27\t\\begin{document}\n    28\t\\maketitle\n    29\t\n    30\t\\begin{abstract}\n    31\tPrediction regions for compositional responses must account for both the simplex constraint and variation in predictive uncertainty. We study whether scale-adaptive split-conformal regions in isometric-logratio coordinates improve reliability and efficiency together. A frozen training-only procedure combines ridge centers, local residual-moment scaling, and nested rank-based selection; independent calibration determines the final regions. Across logistic-normal, Dirichlet, and shifted-Dirichlet settings at two dimensions and eight held-out seeds, the results are condition-dependent. Mean absolute coverage error is 0.0136 under IID sampling but 0.1303 under shift. Aggregate utility exceeds the strongest standard comparator by 0.0116, with a descriptive paired 95\\% seed-bootstrap interval of [0.0047, 0.0179], yet mean uniform-simplex region mass is larger: 0.3035 versus 0.2917. Gains concentrate in higher-dimensional IID Dirichlet responses, whereas shifted comparisons favor the simplex-coordinate baseline. These findings support separating observed-response coverage, reference-measure efficiency, and distributional assumptions rather than interpreting an aggregate utility gain as uniform dominance or conditional validity.\n    32\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n    33\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/paired_overall/simplex_linf]]\n    34\t\\end{abstract}\n    35\t\n    36\t\\section{Introduction}\n    37\tCompositional predictions arise whenever the response is a nonnegative vector constrained to sum to one. A region around such a prediction should respect the constraint, cover the relevant future observation, and remain informative. These requirements are distinct. A small region in transformed coordinates need not occupy little of the simplex; a marginally calibrated region need not be reliable within a feature-defined population; and reliable IID behavior need not persist after a change in covariate distribution. The target itself also matters: uncertainty for an observed continuous composition is not the same problem as a set of possible class labels or a confidence region for an unobserved probability vector.\n    38\t\n    39\tSplit conformal prediction supplies a natural starting point because its marginal coverage guarantee separates fitting a predictive score from calibrating its threshold \\citep{Papadopoulos2002,Lei2018,Angelopoulos2021}. Compositional geometry nevertheless leaves important evaluation choices. Logratio representations remove the sum constraint but change how region size is measured \\citep{Aitchison1982,Egozcue2003}. Heteroscedastic scaling can redistribute widths without altering the calibration rule. Consequently, judging a region construction requires more than a single coverage number or a single transformed-space volume.\n    40\t\n    41\tWe ask: \\emph{how do training-only scale-adaptive logratio regions trade observed-composition coverage against uniform-simplex reference mass, relative to alternatives sharing the same fitted center?} We evaluate one fixed construction rather than claiming a comprehensive comparison of conformal methods. Its scale correction uses local moments of cross-fitted standardized residuals, with a neutral option and nested training-only selection. The final calibration sample is not used to choose that correction. The comparison includes unscaled logratio regions, a joint simplex-coordinate maximum-residual region, and coordinatewise Bonferroni regions.\n    42\t\n    43\tThe empirical answer is not uniform improvement. The scale-adaptive construction has higher aggregate utility but larger aggregate simplex mass than the simplex-coordinate baseline. The most favorable contrast occurs for IID Dirichlet responses in the larger dimension; shifted conditions provide clear adverse comparisons. Thus the useful contribution is a bounded evaluation of a specified configuration and its failure modes, not a new distribution-free guarantee. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json]]\n    44\t\n    45\tOur contributions are: (i) an inspectable training-only scale-adaptation specification with independent finite-rank calibration; (ii) a paired synthetic assessment separating coverage, reference mass, and their utility summary; and (iii) a seed-level uncertainty analysis that retains unfavorable condition-specific comparisons and distinguishes scaling effects from untested selector-specific explanations. The scope is strictly positive continuous responses under the stated generators. Structural zeros, latent probability recovery, real-data performance, and universal geometric preferences are outside the evidence.\n    46\t\n    47\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    48\t\\centering\n    49\t\\includegraphics[width=.98\\textwidth,height=1.6in,keepaspectratio]{figures/composition_reliability_overview.png}\n    50\t\\caption{Study overview. Training data determine centers and local scales, including internal rank-based selection. A separate calibration sample determines conformal thresholds. Evaluation distinguishes membership of observed compositions from mass under a uniform-simplex reference distribution; neither estimand establishes conditional or shifted validity.}\n    51\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    52\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py]]\n    53\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py]]\n    54\t\\end{figure}\n    55\t\n    56\t\\section{Related Work}\n    57\t\\paragraph{Calibration and adaptation.}\n    58\tThe conformal framework provides finite-sample predictive coverage under exchangeability \\citep{Vovk2005}; inductive constructions separate model fitting and calibration \\citep{Papadopoulos2002}. Regression studies compare the statistical and computational behavior of full and split conformal inference \\citep{Lei2018}, while jackknife+ uses leave-one-out predictions to account for fitting variability \\citep{Barber2021Jackknife}. Here the calibration procedure is ordinary split conformal: internal cross-fitting estimates scale information but is not jackknife+ calibration. Locally adaptive intervals have been developed through quantile regression \\citep{Romano2019,Sesia2020}, conditional density estimation \\citep{Lei2014,Izbicki2020}, and localized conformal constructions \\citep{Guan2023}. These motivate adaptation without establishing that our particular scalar correction is optimal. Selection and aggregation raise a separate issue from calibrating one fixed score \\citep{Yang2024}; our fitting choices are confined to training data.\n    59\t\n    60\t\\paragraph{What coverage does not imply.}\n    61\tConditional validity requires care even for inductive predictors \\citep{Vovk2012}. Distribution-free conditional predictive inference has fundamental limits \\citep{Barber2021Limits}, so pooled coverage should not be promoted to a feature-conditional guarantee. Under covariate shift, weighted conformal methods use covariate likelihood ratios \\citep{Tibshirani2019}; broader departures from exchangeability require their own analysis \\citep{Barber2023Beyond}. Adaptive conformal inference instead studies an online coverage-frequency target \\citep{Gibbs2021}. These extensions are not implemented here. Our shifted evaluation is a stress test of a fixed, unweighted calibration rule, not a comparison with shift-aware methods.\n    62\t\n    63\t\\paragraph{Composition and statistical measurement.}\n    64\tCompositional analysis treats ratios and the simplex as central objects \\citep{Aitchison1982,Pawlowsky2015}; logistic-normal distributions provide transformed-normal models on that space \\citep{Aitchison1980}. Isometric logratio (ILR) coordinates give an orthonormal representation \\citep{Egozcue2003}, but orthonormality in logratio geometry does not preserve uniform-simplex mass. Our centers use ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970}, and the scale correction is a shrunk kernel regression of residual moments in the spirit of \\citet{Nadaraya1964}. Uncertainty reporting uses resampling across independent seed blocks \\citep{Efron1979,Davison1997} and distinguishes those intervals from within-sample Wilson coverage intervals \\citep{Brown2001}. The contribution is the resulting controlled audit, not a claim to originate any of these ingredients.\n    65\t\n    66\t\\section{Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets}\n    67\t\\label{sec:regions}\n    68\t\\subsection{Problem setup and calibration}\n    69\tLet $X\\in\\R^6$ and $Y\\in\\simplex=\\{y\\in(0,1)^K:\\sum_k y_k=1\\}$. We predict a region $C_\\alpha(X)$ for an independent \\emph{observed response} $Y$, at target coverage $1-\\alpha$. No class label or latent multinomial parameter is evaluated. A fixed Helmert matrix $B\\in\\R^{K\\times(K-1)}$ satisfies $B^\\top B=I$ and $B^\\top\\mathbf 1=0$; the representation is\n    70\t\\begin{equation}\n    71\t z(y)=B^\\top\\log y,\\qquad h(z)=\\operatorname{softmax}(Bz).\n    72\t\\end{equation}\n    73\tFitted center $\\widehat\\mu(x)\\in\\R^{K-1}$ and positive scales $\\widehat s_j(x)$ define the nonconformity score\n    74\t\\begin{equation}\n    75\t A(x,y)=\\max_{1\\leq j<K}\\frac{|z_j(y)-\\widehat\\mu_j(x)|}{\\widehat s_j(x)}.\n    76\t \\label{eq:score}\n    77\t\\end{equation}\n    78\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_basis]]\n    79\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\n    80\tGiven $m$ independent calibration scores, use the $r$th smallest score with $r=\\lceil(m+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, or $+\\infty$ if $r>m$. The region consists of positive compositions satisfying $A(x,y)\\leq q_\\alpha$. It is an axis-aligned box in the fixed ILR basis, mapped back to the simplex; it is not a full-covariance ellipsoid. The finite-rank rule, including its infinite-threshold case, is owned by the evaluation code rather than the fitted predictor. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#quantile]]\n    81\t\n    82\tConditional on the training data, exchangeable calibration and test scores give $\\Pr\\{Y\\in C_\\alpha(X)\\}\\geq1-\\alpha$ by the standard conformal rank argument \\citep{Lei2018}. Ties are handled conservatively by the non-strict membership inequality. This is a statement about a random calibration sample and future response, not a guarantee for every realized test set, every fixed feature vector, or every group. Training-only adaptation leaves this argument intact because the final calibration responses do not enter score selection. The argument does not apply unchanged to our shifted test sample.\n    83\t\n    84\t\\subsection{Reliability, mass, and utility}\n    85\tFor each fitted instance, empirical coverage $\\widehat c_\\alpha$ is the fraction of test responses inside the region. Reliability error is $e_\\alpha=|\\widehat c_\\alpha-(1-\\alpha)|$, treating overcoverage and undercoverage symmetrically. Efficiency is the normalized uniform-simplex reference mass\n    86\t\\begin{equation}\n    87\t M_\\alpha=\\mathbb E_{X_{\\rm test}}\\Pr_{V\\sim\\operatorname{Dirichlet}(\\mathbf1)}\n    88\t \\{V\\in C_\\alpha(X_{\\rm test})\\}.\n    89\t \\label{eq:mass}\n    90\t\\end{equation}\n    91\tThe implementation estimates this probability with shared probes and test contexts, not by integrating ILR-box volume. Thus $M_\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ is dimension-normalized simplex volume, not probability content under the response law. We use ``mass'' to keep the reference distribution explicit. Small mass alone does not establish informative calibrated prediction. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#generate_arrays]]\n    92\t\n    93\tThe supplied utility combines the two empirical quantities:\n    94\t\\begin{equation}\n    95\t U_\\alpha=(1-\\widehat M_\\alpha)\n    96\t \\max\\left\\{0,1-\\frac{e_\\alpha}{1-\\alpha}\\right\\}.\n    97\t \\label{eq:utility}\n    98\t\\end{equation}\n    99\tIt rewards small regions and penalizes coverage error but is not a coverage constraint. A method can improve $U_\\alpha$ by trading mass against error; its ordering need not agree with either component. We therefore report all components and treat utility as a supplied descriptive summary, not a proof of scientific superiority. Overall utility averages conditions and nominal levels equally. IID and shifted errors are averaged separately, after taking absolute deviations within each seed, condition, and level. Taking the absolute value after averaging coverage would produce a different estimand. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#aggregate]]\n   100\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
